-- title:
--   Gotzmann persistence for maximal Macaulay growth
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $n$ and $m$ with $1 \le m$, a field $K$, and an ideal $J$ of the polynomial ring $K[x_0,\dots,x_n]$ in $n+1$ variables. For a degree $d$, `piece J d` denotes the quotient of the $K$-module of forms of degree $d$ (the degree-$d$ homogeneous submodule of the polynomial ring) by the submodule of those forms that lie in $J$; thus its $K$-dimension is the value at $d$ of the Hilbert function of $J$. Assume that $J$ is generated, as an ideal, by a set $s$ of polynomials each of which is homogeneous of degree $m$, and assume the growth condition $$\dim_K \mathrm{piece}\,J\,(m+1) = \mathrm{macaulayPow}\,m\,\bigl(\dim_K \mathrm{piece}\,J\,m\bigr),$$ where [`Nat.macaulayPow`](def/Nat_MacaulayPow.html#L7) is Macaulay's upper pseudo-power, defined by recursion on its first argument: $\mathrm{macaulayPow}\,0\,a = 0$, and for $d+1$, setting $k$ to be the greatest $k \le a+d+1$ with $\binom{k}{d+1} \le a$, $\mathrm{macaulayPow}\,(d+1)\,a = \binom{k+1}{d+2} + \mathrm{macaulayPow}\,d\,\bigl(a - \binom{k}{d+1}\bigr)$. The conclusion is that the same equality holds in every degree $e \ge m$: $\dim_K \mathrm{piece}\,J\,(e+1) = \mathrm{macaulayPow}\,e\,(\dim_K \mathrm{piece}\,J\,e)$.
--
--   This is Gotzmann's persistence theorem: for an ideal generated by forms of a single degree $m$, maximal Macaulay growth of the Hilbert function from degree $m$ to degree $m+1$ forces maximal growth in all subsequent degrees, so that the Hilbert function agrees with a polynomial from degree $m$ onwards. It is used in the treatment of Hilbert functors and projective space, in particular in the construction of sections by Euler-characteristic counts and in membership criteria for such ideals.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPolynomial_forall_finrank_piece_succ_eq_macaulayPow_of_finrank_piece_succ_eq_macaulayPow.lean

